###############################################################################
# 02 — Users, invitations and self-registration
#
# There is no POST /api/v1/users. An account comes into existence in exactly
# three ways: bootstrap (the owner), an invitation that somebody redeems, or
# self-registration when the deployment has it switched on. There is also no
# user-delete endpoint — accounts are archived, never erased.
#
# NEVER COMMIT A REAL CREDENTIAL OR TOKEN TO THIS FILE.
# Everything below is a placeholder.
###############################################################################

@baseUrl = http://localhost:8090
@accessToken = REPLACE_WITH_ACCESS_TOKEN

@userId = 00000000-0000-7000-8000-000000000001
@invitationId = 00000000-0000-7000-8000-000000000002
@roleId = 00000000-0000-7000-8000-000000000003
@departmentId = 00000000-0000-7000-8000-000000000004
@clientAccountId = 00000000-0000-7000-8000-000000000005

# The optimistic-concurrency token, read from the resource you are about to
# change. Send back the value you actually read — a guess is a 409.
@userVersion = 1

# Any 8–200 printable-ASCII string, unique per logical create. A UUID is fine.
@idempotencyKey = REPLACE_WITH_A_FRESH_UUID_PER_CREATE


### List users
# Cursor-paginated. `limit` defaults to 25 and caps at 100; `sort` is an
# allowlist per endpoint, not a column name.
# Correct response: 200 {items: [...], next_cursor, has_more}. Note there is no
# `total` — computing one means re-scanning the whole matching set on every
# request.
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/users?limit=25&sort=created_at&direction=desc&status=ACTIVE&principal_type=INTERNAL
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Accept: application/json


### Search users
# `search` is a case-insensitive substring over the normalised email and the
# display name. Correct response: 200 with a (possibly empty) page.
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/users?search=alice&limit=10
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Accept: application/json


### Follow a cursor
# Paste `next_cursor` from the previous page. The cursor is opaque and keyset
# based, so page 10 000 costs the same as page 1. It is not signed and is not a
# security boundary — a forged cursor can only reposition a query you were
# already allowed to run.
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/users?limit=25&cursor=REPLACE_WITH_NEXT_CURSOR
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Accept: application/json


### Read one user
# Correct response: 200. `email_normalized` is deliberately absent, and no
# response type in this module has a field capable of holding a password hash.
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/users/{{userId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Accept: application/json


### Update a user
# Only `display_name` and `email` are writable. `principal_type`, `status`,
# `security_version`, `mfa_required` and role membership are not fields of this
# request at all.
# Correct response: 200 with the updated user and an incremented `version`.
# A stale `version` is 409 VERSION_CONFLICT — re-read and retry.
PATCH {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/users/{{userId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json
Accept: application/json

{
  "version": {{userVersion}},
  "display_name": "Alice Example"
}


### Suspend a user
# Correct response: 200 with status SUSPENDED; the subject's sessions are
# revoked. Targeting the system owner is 403 ROOT_PROTECTED.
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/users/{{userId}}/suspend
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json
Accept: application/json

{
  "version": {{userVersion}},
  "reason": "Investigating a reported credential compromise"
}


### Reactivate a user
# Correct response: 200 with status ACTIVE. An ARCHIVED account is not
# reactivated here: that transition is 409.
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/users/{{userId}}/reactivate
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json
Accept: application/json

{
  "version": {{userVersion}}
}


### Archive a user
# The terminal state, and the closest thing to deletion this API offers. The row
# survives so historical references and the meaning of the audit log survive
# with it. There is no DELETE /api/v1/users/{id} and the runtime database role
# holds no DELETE grant on the users table at all.
# Correct response: 200 with status ARCHIVED.
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/users/{{userId}}/archive
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json
Accept: application/json

{
  "version": {{userVersion}},
  "reason": "Left the company"
}


### List invitations
# Correct response: 200, a page of invitations. No projection anywhere carries
# the invitation token or its digest — the token exists only in the email.
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/invitations?status=PENDING&limit=25
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Accept: application/json


### Invite an internal colleague  — idempotent
# The only path to an INTERNAL account. The role set is checked against the
# INVITER's own authority now and again at acceptance, so a stale invitation
# cannot outlive the authority that created it.
#
# `Idempotency-Key` makes the retry safe: same key + same body replays the
# stored response; same key + DIFFERENT body is 409 IDEMPOTENCY_KEY_REUSED.
# Keys are scoped per principal, so one caller's key can never replay another's.
#
# Correct response: 201 with the invitation and an email dispatched. Inviting an
# address that already has a live invitation is 409 — one live invitation per
# address, deterministically.
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/invitations
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json
Accept: application/json
Idempotency-Key: {{idempotencyKey}}

{
  "email": "new.colleague@example.com",
  "display_name": "New Colleague",
  "principal_type": "INTERNAL",
  "role_ids": ["{{roleId}}"],
  "department_id": "{{departmentId}}",
  "client_account_id": null
}


### Invite an external client contact  — idempotent
# A CLIENT invitation carries a client account and never a department; the
# database refuses the other combination outright.
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/invitations
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json
Accept: application/json
Idempotency-Key: REPLACE_WITH_A_DIFFERENT_FRESH_UUID

{
  "email": "contact@client.example.com",
  "display_name": "Client Contact",
  "principal_type": "CLIENT",
  "role_ids": [],
  "department_id": null,
  "client_account_id": "{{clientAccountId}}"
}


### Revoke a pending invitation
# Correct response: 204. Revocation is a status change, not a row deletion. An
# already accepted invitation cannot be revoked: 409.
DELETE {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/invitations/{{invitationId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}


### Redeem an invitation  — ANONYMOUS
# No Authorization header: the invitee has no account yet. The token is in the
# BODY, never in the path or query.
# Correct response: 200 with the new account, and NO session and NO token — the
# invitee logs in through the ordinary path. An unknown or expired token is 404.
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/invitations/accept
Content-Type: application/json
Accept: application/json

{
  "token": "REPLACE_WITH_INVITATION_TOKEN_FROM_EMAIL",
  "password": "REPLACE_WITH_A_LONG_PASSPHRASE",
  "display_name": "New Colleague"
}


### Is self-registration open?  — ANONYMOUS
# Correct response: 200 with exactly two fields, e.g.
# {"registration_available": false, "registration_type": null}. It must not
# disclose the configured mode name.
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/registration/config
Accept: application/json


### Self-register  — ANONYMOUS
# Available only when `registration.mode` is CLIENT_SELF_REGISTRATION, and it
# can only ever create a CLIENT principal in PENDING status with no client
# memberships — so it grants visibility of nothing until somebody internal acts.
# There is no field here for `principal_type`, `role_ids` or `status`.
# Correct response: 202 with a fixed constant that does not reveal whether the
# address was already registered.
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/registration
Content-Type: application/json
Accept: application/json

{
  "email": "prospect@client.example.com",
  "display_name": "Prospective Client",
  "password": "REPLACE_WITH_A_LONG_PASSPHRASE"
}
